  "step_3": "The decision reason requires identifying the least probable outcome (i.e., the outcome with the lowest probability) in each lottery and comparing the value of the most favorable outcome associated with that low-probability event. In Lottery A, the least probable outcome is 0 Euros with 1% probability. In Lottery B, the only outcome is 2400 Euros with 100% probability — so there is no low-probability outcome. Therefore, the concept of a 'least probable outcome' in B is not applicable. The decision reason cannot be applied to this problem because one lottery (B) has no low-probability outcome. Thus, the reason is not applicable. The individual's justification focuses on the risk of getting nothing (0€) being very small and the chance of getting 100€ more being high — which is not about comparing the favorable outcome in the least probable scenario. Instead, it reflects a different reasoning — possibly about risk aversion or perceived gains. Therefore, the individual did not use the decision reason. The information considered by the individual does not match the required information (least probable outcome comparison). The justification does not follow the rule of the decision reason. Hence, the decision reason was not used.",
  "final_assessment_score": 0
}

